module p_ro_mut_method_gap

// №TBD finding (coordinator add-on, 2026-08-06): `E_POINTER_RO_MUT_METHOD`
// (spec 02-types.md:9700/10497 — "p.method() (mut recv) через *ro T ->
// ❌ E_POINTER_RO_MUT_METHOD") has ZERO emissions anywhere in the compiler
// (confirmed by grep before AND after this window's changes — unrelated to
// №383/№384, NOT touched by this window's fix). Empirically CONFIRMED live
// and unenforced, both on `nova check` (no diagnostic at all, not even
// E_UNSAFE_UNUSED — a mut-method call auto-derefed through a raw pointer
// isn't recognized as needing `unsafe` in the first place) and on
// `nova build` + run: this program prints "1" — the mutation through the
// `ro`-declared pointer `p` silently succeeded on the ORIGINAL binding `x`.
// This is a DIFFERENT root cause from №377 ("what comes back through a
// pointer" — deref garbage): this is "is a MUT method call allowed through
// a RO pointer at all" — never enforced. The existing sibling mechanism
// (`E_POINTER_RO_ASSIGN`, types/mod.rs ~9067-9089) only gates a HARDCODED
// list of raw-pointer intrinsic method names (`write`/`write_at`/…), not
// arbitrary user `mut @method` calls — extending that same gate to consult
// the resolved callee's `Receiver.mutable` is the likely fix shape, but NOT
// implemented in this window (separate mechanism — pointer safety, not
// generic-bound checking — out of scope for №383/№384; flagging per
// explicit coordinator request to report, not fix).

type Counter { mut v int }

fn Counter mut @bump() -> @ { @v += 1 }

fn main() Io -> () {
    mut x = Counter { v: 0 }
    ro p *Counter = &x
    p.bump()
    print("${x.v}")
}
